    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, when dealing with non-linear noise spikes, it's crucial to employ robust statistical methods and outlier detection algorithms. One approach is to use a moving average filter, which can help smooth out short-term fluctuations and highlight longer-term trends in the data. For more sophisticated analysis, consider applying a Kalman filter, which is particularly effective in dealing with noisy data and can help estimate the true state of the system by considering both the measurements and the model predictions.\n\nFor outlier detection, you might want to explore algorithms like the Z-score method, which identifies data points that are several standard deviations away from the mean, or the Interquartile Range (IQR) method, which focuses on the middle 50% of the data and flags points that fall outside the expected range. Machine learning techniques, such as Isolation Forest or DBSCAN clustering, can also be useful for identifying and removing anomalies from your dataset.\n\nRemember, it's important to visualize your data before and after applying these methods to ensure that you're not inadvertently removing important signals along with the noise. By combining these techniques with a thorough understanding of the underlying physical processes, you can effectively filter out the non-linear noise spikes and refine your thermohaline models.",
      "source": "target",
